  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 700 PM EDT Tuesday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Dry and seasonable conditions continue through \nWednesday night, with unseasonably hot weather and chances for \nshowers and storms returning later this week into the weekend.\n\nThis evening, a ~1030mb high pressure was centered over New \nEngland, ridging southward into the local area. A refreshingly \ndry and comfortable airmass has settled over the region, with \ntemps in the 70s and dew points in the 50s as of 645 PM. High \npressure settles over the area tonight, leading to light winds, \nmainly clear skies, and another night of excellent radiational \ncooling. Low temperatures will likely drop into the mid-upper \n50s inland, with some pockets of lower 50s possible in the \ntypically cooler spots. Closer to the coast, lows will likely \nrange from the upper 50s to mid 60s. Patchy ground fog is\npossible across the Piedmont and interior portions of the lower\nMD Eastern Shore late tonight into early Wednesday morning.\n\nHigh pressure will begin to settle SW of the local area over the \nCarolinas on Wednesday. Temperatures will be a few degrees warmer on \nWednesday compared to today, ranging from the mid (locally upper \n80s) inland to the upper 70s to lower 80s along the coast under a \nmostly sunny sky. Another cool night Wednesday night with lows \ndipping back into the mid 50s to lower 60s. A warm up commences on \nThursday, along with an influx of slightly more humid air, ahead of \nan approaching cold front from the NW. Seasonably hot on Thursday \nwith highs ranging from the upper 80s to lower 90s inland and low to \nmid 80s along the immediate coast. \n\nThe upper ridge currently centered over the Tennessee Valley well \ncenter further to the SW later this week, allowing for a cold front \nto approach the local area on Friday. Scattered showers and storms \nwill be possible along and ahead of this front, but severe weather \nis not anticipated at this time. The front likely stalls south of \nthe area Friday night into Saturday before lifting back to the north \nas a warm front later in the weekend.",
